Z||000003||fierospeeder||10-21-1999||12:51 AM||||im buying an engine lift to do mine, it is the safest, i seen engine holders that GM has made, its in the owners manual and theres also a diagram of it in the haynes book somewhere in the trans section, its a bar that goes across the engine with two threaded hooks and at the two ends are legs that clamp somewhere on the struts im not sure.  I dont know where you can get one.  Pepboys sells an engine lift for 250 and i will always be needing one instead of renting one.  <BR>For my 2.5, you have to remove the alternator, alternator bracket, the starter, the bolts on the front and back of the engine to remove the bracket, and unbolt the engine mount, and remove some of the flywheel cover.  It takes about a day to do all of this taking it apart and putting it back together, and your neck hurts.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||10-21-1999||07:14 AM||||Dave,<BR>I assume your screen name indicates that you have an 85 V-6.  This is significant since the pan on the 85 is different than the later model engines.  The front of the pan where it meets the timing chain cover is sloped upward and is a bit harder to get to than the later models if you are trying to get to the bolts with the engine still in the car.<BR>I've never tried to remove the pan with the engine still in the car but thinking of mine sitting on the engine stand (I am out of town and am working from memory right now) I can see that you will probably have to remove the front engine mount and raise the engine slightly.  Also the gasket at the rear of the engine that seals the pan around the rear main seal is accessible after you remove the flywheel dust shield.  If I recall correctly you will have to tilt the pan forward to get the splash baffle inside the pan to clear the oil pump as you remove the pan from the engine.<P>I assume you are going to tackle this because of leaks.  The most likely places the pan will leak is at the point where the block and timing chain cover come together and where the rear main seal cover gasket meets the block (assuming the pan is not bent anywhere).  Be sure and dab some RTV at these junction points when you place the new gasket.<BR>I"ve never worked on an auto trans car and there could be other problems with clearances around the trans.<P>Good luck,<BR>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000005||Dave85||10-21-1999||12:30 PM||||Thanks for the input! I was wanting to stop the oil leak but it sounds like I can live with it a little longer... until the rebuild in the spring.<BR>Thanks again.  Dave<BR>||||reg||1
